// Automatic Comparison of PSG and Zeo Sleep Stage Data Program
// EA Zavrel            
// Program written APR, MAY, JUN, and JUL 2014, Additions made April 2016

// Notes:

// Program performs automatic comparison of Zeo and PSG sleep stage data sets

// Program loads Zeo and PSG sleep stage data sets, locates and fills gaps in Zeo data and performs interpolation of scored PSG sleep stage data file (converts from minimal file to 
// complete file suitable for stage by stage comparison) before performing comparison between data sets to arrive at percent agreement and kappa

// Zeo drops 0-2 stages per night (1 in 1000). A sleep stage determination is expected every 30 seconds. Occasionally, a sleep stage is dropped resulting in consecutive stages being
// 1 minute apart rather than 30 seconds. Also, while it highly unlikely, it is possible for more than one stage to be dropped in succession, resulting in two consecutive stages being
// separated by more than 1 minute. For instance, if approx. 1 out of every 1000 stages gets dropped, then there is 1 in 1 million chance that two successive sleep stages in a night will be
// dropped. Program accounts for that possibility (gap consisting of more than 1 missed sleep stage). Gap is filled in with a "MISSING" descriptor rather than filling gap with last stage
// value (conservative approach). Were this drop effect not taken into account, data sets would be out of sync by 30 sec (in addition to the minimized misalignment between them), 
// and the data sets would become misaligned by an additional 30 sec every time Zeo dropped a stage.

// File generated by Grass from scored study is a minimal file showing only when stage changes: from minimal file, a  complete file may be generated (reconstructed) by interpolating or filling in the gaps

// Program finds nearest time in PSG sleep stage data to the starting point specified for the Zeo sleep stage data and performs a comparison and evaluation of true, false, or N/A

// Comparison is only made if valid stages are present in both data sets: if Zeo stage is UNK (headband off) or "MISSING" (corresponding to gap) or if PSG stage is "No Stage"(corresponding to 
// disconnection as for bathroom break), then no stage comparison is made

// June 5 2014: After completing analysis of data sets,  found an exception / error was occurring: occasionally the time stamp of a sleep stage is non-standard (not 0 or 30 sec): Will be 29 or 
// 59 seconds instead, indicating that the last time stamp transmission was corrupted and not read (resulting in last valid time stamp value being used). This results in error as program is 
// expecting all sleep stage time stamps to have 0 or 30 for second value. If a sleep stage has a non-standard time stamp, don't attempt to correct it automatically (too difficult). Instead, 
// make changes manually.

// June 6 2014: Found why contingency table generated in program differed slightly from contingency table generated using SPSS: 
// zeo_sleep_stage_description_stage for when PSG sleep stage was light should have said if PSG stage was N1 or N2 but instead read if PSG stage was N1 or N2 or N3 (mistake)

// June 8 2014: Checked program against SPSS: confirmed statistical analysis returns same kappa value and same contingency table
// Formatted / processed PSG sleep descriptions (W, R, N1, N2, N3) to remove excess spaces for superior display

// Jul 1 2014: Added awakening, REM epoch, and night summary files. These files were created for Zeo in real-time but had to be created for post-analysis as time frame is different: 
// subjects often wore Zeo headband for significant time (hours) before being set up for PSG. Need common specified time frame for accurately comparing such things as SOL.

// Jul 2014: Changed formatting for output files

// Confirmed calculations for such things as time in each stage, percent in each stage, number of awakenings and their durations, and number of REM epochs and their durations were correct
// by comparing to values created for Zeo by Arduino data logger. For those data sets were there was a small difference in specified time frame (meaning that subject was set up with PSG and
// Zeo around the same time with), values were same (as only difference would be 10-20 min additional wake time. Calculations for SOL (sleep onset latency) and LPS (latency to persistent
// sleep) can be confirmed by comparing night summary files created by Arduino data logger and Processing post-analysis and accounting for time shift / delay between when Zeo was undocked
// and when PSG recording began.

// "Couldn't create a writer for" certain file error caused by having file open when trying to write to it: files can't be open when trying to write to them

// Jul 8 2014: Corrected calculation of total recording time (TRT): was underestimating by 30 sec. Previously, defined TRT as difference between timestamps of first and last stages. However,
// stage determination is taken to apply to the 30 sec following the associated timestamp so last stage applies to last timestamp plus 30 sec. Added correction factor (+ 30 sec) to yield 
// correct TRT.

// Jul 9 2014: Added feature to show correlation (if any) between sleep fragmentation and Zeo accuracy. Hypothesis is that Zeo will be more accurate with less fragmented sleep and less
// accurate with more fragmented / disturbed sleep. Feature added that calculates average time between stage transitions (average time spent in single stage). As intended as a measure of 
// degree of fragmentation of sleep, excludes wake time before sleep and final wake time (from final waking to end of recording). Confirmed correct calculation: same amount of time obtained
// by multiplying average time between transitions by number of unique blocks (between transitions) as between sleep onset and final waking minus any unknown, no stage, or missing stages.

// Solved kappa calculation discrepancy issue between program and SPSS: had not updated a couple (N1 and N2) psg variable names in comparison section (when removed white space from psg stage
// names), e.g., "N1      " to "N1" or "N2      " to "N2." Now program and SPSS yield same results (same number of comparisons, same kappa)

// Example checks to confirm calculations are correct: 

// Sum of awakenings (after sleep onset and before final awakening) = WASO (excludes time to sleep onset and time spent in final awakening)
// If no unknown or missing stages, then TWT + TST = TRT
// If missing / unknown stages, then TWT + TST + UNK + MISSING = TRT
// TST = REM + L + D 

// Note: Sum of Zeo wake stages from contingency table (Zeo W / PSG W + Zeo W / PSG REM + Zeo W / PSG L + Zeo W / PSG D) may be less than total wake time (TWT) in night summary file as 
// contingency table is only added to when both Zeo and PSG stages are present and defined. So if Zeo stage is W and PSG stage is "No Stage," then this comparison is omitted from contingency
// table as no comparison is possible and TWT in night summary file will be 1 stage or 30 sec longer than sum of Zeo wake stages in contingency table.

// May also be true for total time in a particular stage. Zeo may be in Light while PSG reports "No Stage." Therefore, time in a particular stage as obtained from contingency table may differ 
// from time in same stage as determined by program count.

// PSG "No Stage" - when patient is disconnected from headbox for bathroom break but also when signal is bad and no stage can be assigned (sometimes only No Stage for 1 stage or 30 sec - too
// short to represent a bathroom break.

// Jul 11 2014: Made change to where measure / degree of sleep fragmentation for PSG data is calculated. When using PSG stage and PSG stage old, was counting N1 / N2 transitions as a
// stage change when for this comparison, they are both considered same stage (Light). Made change to use SPSS description instead. This resulted in a longer average time between transitions 
// as N1 to N2 transitions are no longer seen as a stage change. Confirmed counting time between transitions correctly (block number and time between transitions output to debug terminal / 
// serial terminal and manually compared them against boolean comparison file). Match confirmed. Ignores time to sleep onset and final awakening. Interested in sleep fragmentation. 
// Conservative approach / measure used rather than reduced sleep fragmentation measure by counting wake time before and after sleep.

// Had to correct LPS calculation: was underestimating by 30 sec. Was counting time to start of 10th min (20th epoch) of continuous sleep as end point rather than the time to end of 10th min
// (20th epoch) of continuous sleep as end point. SOL and REML do not need to be changed as counting time to start of those rather than end of a block (inclusive).

// Jul 12 2014: Added compilation files to which output values get appended to avoid laborious task of manually compiling output values by copying + pasting:
// data entry is time consuming + prone to error

// Jul 15 2014: NEED TO ADD FEATURE TO ACCOUNT FOR IF NO SLEEP ONSET OCCURS OR NO REM ONSET OCCURS (AS DETECTED BY ZEO AND PSG)!!! 
// RIGHT NOW, PROGRAM ASSUMES BOTH SLEEP ONSET AND REM ONSET WILL OCCUR AND CALCULATES VALUES FOR SOL AND REML - THIS IS AN ASSUMPTION!
// NEED TO PUT N/A IF THESE ONSETS DO NOT OCCUR RATHER THAN CALCULATE ERRONEOUS VALUES (TIME AT WHICH RECORDING STARTS - TIME OF ONSET = SOMETHING MINUS ZERO) FOR SOL AND REML

// Jun 9 2015: Added feature to ensure SOL, LPS, REML, and WASO values only added to files if sleep onset, persistent sleep, and REM actually occur: if not, then value of -1 appears to signify N/A
// Added feature to ensure % L, D, REM are calculated only if sleep onset occurs; otherwise, total sleep time = 0 and would be dividing zero by zero. 
// If sleep onset does not occur, -1 is used for % L, D, REM to signify N/A
// Corrected REM discrepency (between contingency table count / tally and REM epoch files: previously, if comparison ended in the middle of a REM epoch, last epoch did not get added to REM file.

// June 22 2015: 
// Line 4415: REM block analysis - need to determine if analysis ends in REM block
// Line 4839: Awakening block analysis - omitting final awakening is good

// April 6 2016:
// Added REM block analysis: 30s, 60s, 90s, 120s duration blocks ... to determine threshold duration for certain accuracy, looks for contingency of terminating analysis during a REM block
// Still need to add termination in REM block feature for epoch by epoch TRUE / FALSE analysis file! LINE 4691 DONE
// Need to add compilation feature (load from file into array and then write back) to append each data set instead of overwriting DONE
// When computing percent agreement, need to define number of epochs in agreement and total number of epochs as float variable types and not int. Defining as int will yield result of 0.0:
// 1 / 10 will yield 0.0 while 1.0 / 10.0 will yield 0.1

// April 26 2016:
// REM block analysis: 
// Need to change order of nested loops from if Zeo ... if PSG to ... if PSG ... if Zeo:
// if Zeo ... if PSG: will give lower % agreement as Zeo tends to overestimate REM sleep (low specificity)
// if PSG ... if Zeo will give higher % agreement as Zeo as has high accuracy for REM but low specificity (Zeo detects REM when it is there but has many false positives)
// This is confirmed by contingency table giving Zeo a high % agreement (80%) but a lower % agreement using if Zeo ... if PSG REM block analysis (PPV 50-60%)

// June 9 2016:
// Changed REM block analysis so now looking at percent agreement (if PSG says it is REM, see what Zeo says rather than if Zeo says it is REM, see what PSG says)
// Changed awakening block analysis so now looking at percent agreement (if PSG says it is an awakening, see what Zeo says rather than if Zeo says it is an awakening, see what PSG says)

// Stage Equivalency / Translation Key:

// Zeo | PSG
//
// AWK | W
// REM | R
// L   | N1 or N2
// D   | N3

// Program performs multiple comparisons of varying degrees of rigor:

// 1. Stage by stage (Wake, REM, Light, Deep)
// Match / Equivalency:

// AWK | W
// REM | R
// L   | N1 or N2
// D   | N3

// 2. Wake, REM, and NREM sleep (L and D = NREM)
// Match / Equivalency:

// AWK | W
// REM | R
// L   | N1, N2, or N3
// D   | N1, N2, or N3

// 3. Wake and Sleep (REM, L, D or REM and NREM = Sleep)
// Match / Equivalency:

// AWK | W
// REM | N2, N2, N3 or R
// L   | N2, N2, N3 or R
// D   | N2, N2, N3 or R

// PSG Data Export Instructions:

// 1.	Open Grass Database Explorer
// 2.	Select desired record and double click on PSG to open sleep study
// 3.	Click on “Notation list” icon at top, far right
// 4.	Click “Clear all”
// 5.	Expand PSG Events and select only “Stage”
// 6.	With Notation List window open, go File -> Export Twin Data
// 7.	Select “Export Data to Microsoft Word”
// 8.	Notation data opens automatically in Word document
// 9.	Remove header and footers that automatically get inserted by deleting top empty row (header offset) and shifting entire data set up one line
//      Delete trailing two lines so cursor is on same line as last line of data: by doing this, each line in array will contain data and there will be no need to remove header and footer
// 10.	File save and name file: select .txt file extension
// 11.	File conversion window will open: select OK
// 12.	In blank Excel sheet, go to Data, click on "Get External Data," and select “From Text”
// 13.	Data Import Wizard will open
// 14.	Select “Delimited” not “Fixed width”
// 15.	Click Next
// 16.	Make sure only “Comma” is selected under Delimiters
// 17.	Click Next
// 18.  For column data format, select "General" for column 1, "Text" for column 2, "Text" for column 3
// 19.	Click Finish
// 20.	Click OK
// 21.	File save and name file: select .CSV file extension
// 22.	Click OK
// 23.	Click Yes
// 24.  Close file
// 25.  Click Yes to save changes
// 26.  Click Save
// 27.  Select Yes to replace existing file 
// 28.  Click Yes

